@@ -7,6 +7,30 @@ the applicable site class** — the ``Fa``/``Fv`` site-coefficient tables of
 plus a site class to ``SMS``/``SM1`` inside this library, and this module does
 not pretend otherwise: you supply site-adjusted values.

+Commentary C11.4.3 is explicit about why: ``SMS`` and ``SM1`` (and ``SDS``,
+``SD1``) now incorporate site effects themselves, "eliminating the need for
+the tables of site factors Fa and Fv of ASCE 7-16". The strings ``Table
+11.4-1`` and ``Table 11.4-2`` do not occur anywhere in 7-22. Do not add them
+here — a 7-16 ``Fa``/``Fv`` applied to 7-22 mapped values is neither edition.
+
+**If all you have is a location.** Ask the ASCE 7 Hazard Tool
+(https://asce7hazardtool.online/, or the USGS Seismic Design Web Service) for
+the site class you are designing to; it returns ``SMS``/``SM1`` and the
+multi-period ordinates for the same latitude/longitude that would have given
+you ``Ss``/``S1``. The Chapter 22 printed maps likewise plot ``SMS`` and
+``SM1`` for the default site conditions of §11.4.2.1, not ``Ss``/``S1`` to be
+multiplied by a coefficient.
+
+**If your ``Ss``/``S1`` came from a 7-16 or IBC job**, they are 7-16 mapped
+values on a different reference site condition, and the correct object for
+them is :class:`~codeSpectra.codes.asce7.ASCE7_16`, which owns the 7-16
+``Fa``/``Fv`` tables. Building a 7-22 spectrum out of them is a mixed-edition
+result that no clause authorises.
+
+``Ss``/``S1`` remain accepted here as optional metadata: they are recorded in
+the report and enable the §11.6 ``S1 >= 0.75`` seismic-design-category
+override. They are never multiplied by anything.
+
 Two construction paths mirror the two the standard defines:

